declare(strict_types=1);
echo '<div class="mk-prose">';
echo '<p class="mk-muted" style="margin-top:0;">A thesis on Igbo time-reckoning — why the Igbo count nights, what this reveals about the soul\'s journey, and how it resolves the most persistent anomaly in the Christian resurrection narrative.</p>';

echo '<h2>The Claim</h2>';
echo '<p>The Igbo people count time in nights — <em>abalị</em> — not days. This is not a quirk of language or a primitive approximation of Gregorian time. It is a coherent metaphysical position: a statement about the nature of existence, the direction of the soul\'s journey, and the relationship between the visible and invisible worlds. When this position is understood, it resolves one of the most persistent and least satisfactorily explained anomalies in the Christian gospel narrative — the question of how Jesus could have been in the tomb for "three days and three nights" when the timeline of the gospels appears to allow only two nights and one full day between the crucifixion on Friday and the resurrection on Sunday morning.</p>';
echo '<p>The resolution is not a Christian theological argument. It is an Igbo cosmological observation. And its implications extend far beyond the resurrection narrative into the deepest questions of what time is, where human existence is located within it, and what it means to say that the soul enters and leaves the world.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#2d6a1f;">⊕ Why Nights? The Cosmological Argument</h2>';

echo '<h3>Existence Begins in Darkness</h3>';
echo '<p>In Igbo cosmology, the soul originates in the spirit world — <em>ụwa mmụọ</em> — the world of the ancestors and the not-yet-born. This world is not a place of punishment or waiting; it is the primary world, the world from which the living world is a temporary excursion. The soul chooses its life before birth — <em>ọ họrọ onweya chi</em>, the soul selects its own chi — and enters the living world through birth. At death, the soul returns.</p>';
echo '<p>The direction of the soul\'s journey is therefore: from darkness (spirit world) into light (living world) and back into darkness (spirit world). The soul is fundamentally a creature of the night-world, visiting the day-world for a single lifetime. To count time in nights is to count from the soul\'s perspective — from the origin toward the destination and back again. Days are the visitor\'s measure; nights are the resident\'s measure.</p>';
echo '<p>This is why the Igbo say <em>ọ bụ abalị, ọ bụghị ụbọchị</em> — "it is a night, not a day." The night is the primary unit. The day is what happens inside the night, not the other way around.</p>';

echo '<h3>The Womb as Night</h3>';
echo '<p>In Igbo understanding, the unborn child is in darkness — in the night of the womb, which is continuous with the darkness of the spirit world. Birth is the emergence from night into light: the child crosses from the invisible world into the visible world, from the night-world into the day-world. The naming ceremony on the fourth day — the fourth market day after birth, following the four-night cycle — marks the child\'s formal reception into the community of the living. Four nights must pass before the child is acknowledged as having committed to the living world.</p>';
echo '<p>Death reverses this: the person returns into the night. The burial — traditionally inside the compound, in the earth (Ala\'s body) — is the return to the dark ground from which the soul will eventually re-emerge. The second burial (<em>iwa ọkwa</em>) held weeks or months after the first marks the completion of this transition: the soul has now arrived in the spirit world and taken its place among the ancestors.</p>';

echo '<h3>The Four Nights and the Market Week</h3>';
echo '<p>The Igbo four-day market week — Eke, Orie, Afo, Nkwo — is properly a four-night week. Each night has its own quality, its own spiritual character, its own presiding force. Trading, planting, healing, burying — each activity is scheduled according to the night on which it falls, not the day. The market meets on the day that follows the night, but the night is primary.</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Eke-night</strong> — the night of fire and beginning; the night of new things; the market at dawn that follows is the primary market, the one at which new contracts are made</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Orie-night</strong> — the night of water and feeling; the night of ancestors; the market at dawn is attended by the women\'s societies and by those seeking healing</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Afo-night</strong> — the night of earth and production; the night of the land; the market at dawn is the agricultural market, for seed yams and planting material</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Nkwo-night</strong> — the night of air and spirit; the night of the invisible world; the market at dawn is the night-market in which masquerades may appear and in which the boundary between the living and the dead is most permeable</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<p>The week is four nights. The days are the visible faces of the nights. To say "four days" is to count the faces; to say "four nights" is to count the substance.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#b7791f;">⊕ The Three Nights of Jesus — The Anomaly and Its Resolution</h2>';

echo '<h3>The Problem</h3>';
echo '<p>Jesus himself, in the Gospel of Matthew (12:40), stated: <em>"For as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the great fish, so will the Son of Man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth."</em> This is an explicit prophecy: three days <em>and</em> three nights in the tomb.</p>';
echo '<p>The problem is the timeline. According to all four gospels, Jesus was crucified on Friday (the day before the Sabbath), placed in the tomb before sunset on Friday, and found risen on Sunday morning. Count the nights: Friday night (one), Saturday night (two). Count the days: Saturday (one). That gives, at most, two nights and one full day — not three days and three nights.</p>';
echo '<p>Christian theologians have proposed several solutions:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Inclusive counting</strong> — any part of a day counts as a full day; Friday afternoon counts as "day one," Saturday as "day two," Sunday morning as "day three." This is the most widely accepted explanation, but it requires counting a few hours of Friday afternoon as an entire "day and night," which stretches ordinary language.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Wednesday crucifixion theory</strong> — some scholars argue Jesus was crucified on Wednesday, giving three full nights (Wednesday, Thursday, Friday) and three days (Thursday, Friday, Saturday). But this requires revising the gospel timeline significantly and has not gained mainstream acceptance.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Different calendar</strong> — some argue that the Jewish day began at sunset, making Friday at sunset the beginning of Saturday; this helps somewhat but does not fully resolve the "three nights" count.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<p>None of these solutions is fully satisfying. The "inclusive counting" explanation is the least unsatisfying, but it requires accepting that Jesus was using "day" and "night" in a very loose sense for a prophecy that he stated with apparent precision.</p>';

echo '<h3>The Igbo Resolution</h3>';
echo '<p>In Igbo time-reckoning, where the night is the primary unit, the count works perfectly and naturally:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>Night One</strong> — Friday night (from sunset Friday to sunrise Saturday)</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Night Two</strong> — Saturday night (from sunset Saturday to sunrise Sunday)</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Night Three</strong> — the night that ends at the resurrection: in Igbo reckoning, the period from the last sunset before the resurrection to the moment of resurrection. If the resurrection occurred before dawn on Sunday — as the gospels suggest ("while it was still dark," John 20:1) — then Sunday night had already begun in the Igbo counting system (since the night begins at sunset), and the resurrection occurs during this third night, before its day begins.</li>';
echo '</ul>';
echo '<p>More precisely: in a time-system that counts nights as the primary unit, the sequence runs:</p>';
echo '<ol>';
echo '<li>Crucifixion and burial occur on Friday afternoon — the body enters the earth at the beginning of the first night (Eke-night, the night of the new beginning)</li>';
echo '<li>Saturday is the day inside the second night (Orie-night — the night of water and the ancestral world; the night of the in-between)</li>';
echo '<li>The resurrection occurs at or before dawn on Sunday — during the third night (Afo-night — the night of the earth, of emergence from the ground, of new growth breaking through)</li>';
echo '</ol>';
echo '<p>In this counting, three nights have passed. The statement of Matthew 12:40 is precisely and literally true — not approximately true through inclusive counting, but actually true in a time-system where the night precedes the day and the soul\'s journey is measured in nights.</p>';

echo '<h3>Why This Matters</h3>';
echo '<p>This is not a claim that Jesus or Matthew was using Igbo time-reckoning. It is an observation that the apparent contradiction in the resurrection timeline dissolves when viewed through a time-system in which the night is primary — which is to say, a time-system rooted in the cosmological assumption that existence begins in darkness and moves toward light, rather than beginning in light and ending in darkness.</p>';
echo '<p>The Gregorian day begins at midnight — an arbitrary convention that has no cosmological grounding. The Jewish day begins at sunset — already closer to the Igbo understanding. The Igbo night begins at sunset and is the primary unit of time. In this system, the crucifixion, the burial, and the resurrection are distributed across three complete nights. The prophecy is fulfilled.</p>';
echo '<p>The deeper point is theological: a cosmology in which existence begins in darkness — in the spirit world, in the womb, in the night — produces a natural understanding of three nights in the earth. A cosmology in which existence begins in light — the Enlightenment, the Cartesian day-world of measurable daylight hours — produces the anomaly that has exercised Christian theologians for centuries.</p>';
echo '<p>Igbo time-reckoning is not a curiosity or a primitive survival. It is a coherent cosmological system that encodes, in its most basic unit of measurement, a complete metaphysics of the soul\'s journey. And in doing so, it illuminates something that the dominant time-system of Western Christianity has obscured.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2 style="color:#553c9a;">⊕ Nights in Rites of Passage</h2>';

echo '<p>The use of nights as the primary unit of time is not limited to the market week. It structures the most important transitions in Igbo life:</p>';

echo '<h3>Birth</h3>';
echo '<p>The newborn child is not publicly named until the fourth market day after birth — which in night-reckoning means after four nights have passed. The child has spent four nights in the living world before being formally received into it. This echoes the creation pattern: four nights of darkness before the naming that brings the child fully into the light of community recognition.</p>';

echo '<h3>Marriage</h3>';
echo '<p>The Igbo marriage ceremony — <em>igba nkwu</em> (wine-carrying) — takes place across multiple nights. The negotiations between families, the payment of bride price, the celebration — these are structured around nights, not days. The marriage is not complete in the daylight transaction but in the night of feasting, dancing, and ancestral acknowledgment that follows.</p>';

echo '<h3>Death and Burial</h3>';
echo '<p>The Igbo funeral — <em>ikwa ozu</em> — lasts multiple nights. The second night is traditionally the most significant: the night of wailing, of dancing, of masquerade appearance, of the formal encounter between the living and the dead. The body is buried on a specific day, but the spiritual work of the funeral is done in the nights. The soul\'s passage into the ancestor world is a night-passage — from the darkness of death into the deeper darkness of the spirit world, and eventually into the light of a new birth.</p>';

echo '<h3>Initiation</h3>';
echo '<p>Initiation rites — whether into age grades, titled societies, or dibia practice — involve specified numbers of nights in specific states: nights of seclusion, nights of instruction, nights of ordeal. The initiated person has spent a defined number of nights in the liminal state before emerging transformed. The night is the medium of transformation; the day is the medium of display.</p>';

echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2>Conclusion: Time as Theology</h2>';

echo '<p>How a people counts time is how they understand existence. The Gregorian calendar, with its day as the primary unit and its midnight as the beginning of a new day, encodes a worldview in which the visible, the measurable, and the quantifiable are primary. Time begins in the middle of darkness and moves through light to darkness again — but the darkness is neither the beginning nor the end in any meaningful sense.</p>';
echo '<p>The Igbo night-based calendar encodes a different worldview: existence begins in the spirit world (darkness), moves through the living world (light), and returns to the spirit world (darkness). The night is the primary unit because the spirit world is the primary world. We are visitors in the daylight; we are residents of the night.</p>';
echo '<p>This is not poetry. It is cosmology. And when it is used as a lens through which to read the Christian resurrection narrative, it produces a result that centuries of inclusive-counting and calendar-revision arguments have not produced: a literal, natural, and cosmologically grounded fulfillment of the prophecy of three nights in the earth.</p>';
echo '<p><em>Ọ bụ abalị, ọ bụghị ụbọchị.</em> It is a night, not a day. The Igbo knew this long before Bethlehem.</p>';

